      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "Psoriasis has been suggested to be an independent risk factor for cardiovascular disease (CVD); however, available studies have shown inconsistent results. In this study, embedded within the population-based Rotterdam Study, we aimed to assess the association between psoriasis and cardiovascular outcomes. Adjusted means were calculated for subclinical atherosclerosis using general linear models. Using Cox regression, the hazards of cardiovascular events for psoriasis, as a time-dependent variable, were calculated. A total of 262 psoriasis (24% systemic/UV treatment) and 8,009 reference subjects were followed up for a mean of 11 years. Psoriasis patients were significantly younger, smoked more, and had higher diastolic blood pressure and body mass index levels. The adjusted carotid intima-media thickness was 1.02±0.18\u2009mm for psoriasis and 1.02±0.16\u2009mm for reference subjects. Similarly, crude and adjusted ankle-brachial index, pulse-wave velocity, and coronary artery calcium scores did not differ between the two groups. The risk of incident CVD was not increased in psoriasis (adjusted hazard ratio 0.73, 95% confidence interval 0.50-1.06). The results were similar when coronary heart disease, stroke, and heart failure were analyzed separately. Psoriasis patients with predominantly mild disease from the general population are as likely to develop atherosclerosis and cardiovascular events as subjects without psoriasis."},
